<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
Some attributes accepted by <code>stdenv.mkDerivation</code> are really builder
environment variables. Declaring them as top-level derivation attributes works,
but it blurs the line between structured derivation arguments and environment
customization.
</p>
<p>
In nixpkgs, these variables should instead live under the <code>env</code>
attribute. That keeps environment mutation explicit and matches the convention
enforced by <code>nixpkgs-hammering</code>'s
<code>environment-variables-go-to-env</code> lint.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Move the flagged binding into the <code>env</code> attribute set. For example,
write <code>env = { NIX_CFLAGS_COMPILE = "-O3"; };</code> instead of declaring
<code>NIX_CFLAGS_COMPILE</code> directly at the top level of the derivation.
</p>
<p>
If the current value is a list, rewrite it as an explicit string first.
Unlike top-level derivation attributes, <code>env</code> is intended for
string-valued environment variables.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Problematic — the environment variable is declared at the top level:</p>
<sample language="nix">
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "demo";
  version = "1.0";
  NIX_CFLAGS_COMPILE = "-O3";
}
</sample>
<p>Recommended — move it under <code>env</code>:</p>
<sample language="nix">
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "demo";
  version = "1.0";
  env = {
    NIX_CFLAGS_COMPILE = "-O3";
  };
}
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>
<a href="https://github.com/jtojnar/nixpkgs-hammering/blob/main/explanations/environment-variables-go-to-env.md">
<code>nixpkgs-hammering</code> explanation: <code>environment-variables-go-to-env</code></a>
</li>
<li>
<a href="https://nixos.mayflower.consulting/blog/2020/01/20/structured-attrs/">
Structured attributes in Nixpkgs</a>
</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
